\section{An Order That Reaches Every Word}

Recognition asks a membership question about one word. Enumeration must produce all members, once each, in an order that can actually be followed. In \textit{Enumerating the Strings of Regular Languages}, M.~Douglas McIlroy specifies length order, with ties broken lexicographically. This is often called \emph{shortlex} order. For a finite ordered alphabet it places only finitely many words before any given word. Ordinary dictionary order lacks that property: if $a<b$, then every $a^nb$ with $n\ge1$ precedes $b$. A traversal that keeps choosing the smallest extension may never reach $b$ at all.

McIlroy develops two constructions in Haskell. One interprets expressions directly as ordered sets, using merge for union and ordered operations for concatenation and star. The other constructs a nondeterministic automaton and traverses it breadth first. The direct approach makes the role of the regular operators particularly clear. It is not enough, though, to nest two ordinary loops for concatenation: if the inner operand is infinite, the outer loop never advances. Similarly, for a nullable operand, an implementation of star must avoid endlessly reintroducing the empty word without producing anything new.

\section{Worked Enumeration by Layers}

Hing Leung's \textit{Regular Languages and Finite Automata} and Hopcroft and Ullman's \textit{Formal Languages and Their Relation to Automata} provide the standard automaton constructions underlying the traversal. Here is a deterministic version suited to testing. First mark the states from which at least one accepting state is reachable, by a reverse traversal from all accepting states. Begin with the pair $(\varepsilon,q_0)$, if $q_0$ is marked. Keep a queue of pairs $(w,q)$. Pop its head, emit $w$ if $q$ is accepting, then append $(wa,\delta(q,a))$ in alphabet order for each defined outgoing transition whose target is marked.

Consider the alphabet $a<b$ and the language of words with an even number of $a$'s. A two-state DFA flips on $a$ and stays in place on $b$; only the even state is accepting. At length zero it emits $\varepsilon$. At length one it emits $b$, but still extends $a$, since the odd state can reach an accepting state. At length two the queue visits $aa,ab,ba,bb$ in that order and emits $aa,bb$. The first four outputs are therefore $\varepsilon,b,aa,bb$. A traversal that marks states as globally visited, instead of storing prefixes, would lose $b$ immediately and miss infinitely many words.

The queue invariant is that each pending prefix reaches its paired state, and each pending prefix occurs once. Extending distinct prefixes of the same length in their existing order preserves lexicographic order within the next layer. Pruning preserves completeness because every prefix of an accepted word leads to a marked state. If the language is finite, its prefix set is finite and the queue empties; without pruning, a rejecting sink can keep that queue busy forever. With an NFA one can instead pair each word with its reachable state set, including the appropriate empty-transition closure. Emit the word once if its set contains an accepting state, even if several distinct computations reached accepting states.

\section{Counting Before Generating}

Breadth-first traversal may store exponentially many prefixes at a single depth. If only one word of a given length is needed, repeated suffix counts can replace that frontier. Let $C_k(q)$ be the number of accepted suffixes of length $k$ from state $q$ of a DFA. Then
\[
 C_0(q)=[q\text{ is accepting}],\qquad
 C_{k+1}(q)=\sum_{a\in\Sigma} C_k(\delta(q,a)),
\]
with an absent transition contributing zero. Even if two letters lead to the same state, count both: their suffix blocks have different first letters. The table lets an implementation skip entire lexicographic blocks to select a word by rank. The same sum over individual NFA edges would count paths instead, so determinize or count over reachable subsets first.

For scanner tests, bound the maximum length and record the alphabet; changing either changes what the suite covers. Positive examples alone do not test overacceptance. On a complete DFA, swap accepting and rejecting states to enumerate negative examples, or take a product of two recognizers and accept exactly when one accepts and the other rejects. The first product witness in shortlex order is a shortest reproducible disagreement. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-140} extends this idea to structured test generation, where a derivation and its expected tree matter as much as its terminal string.
